WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.172
<c.steel>Aaron asked for one thing above all:</c>

2
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.556
<c.steel>keep apart what is set in steel, what exists</c>

3
00:00:07.556 --> 00:00:10.801
<c.steel>only as easter eggs, and the running assumptions.</c>

4
00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:12.676
So every line of this lore

5
00:00:12.676 --> 00:00:15.364
wears one of six marks.

6
00:00:15.758 --> 00:00:18.753
<c.steel>Steel is what Aaron has decided. Rosie is</c>

7
00:00:18.753 --> 00:00:21.906
<c.steel>Amara's mother, and Noor's grandmother.</c>

8
00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:24.539
<c.fact>On screen is what you can see or hear</c>

9
00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.848
<c.fact>in a made episode: Rosie turns nine</c>

10
00:00:27.848 --> 00:00:30.246
<c.fact>on the night of Episode 1.</c>

11
00:00:30.646 --> 00:00:34.083
<c.egg>Planted is an egg: the thing is on screen,</c>

12
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:36.480
<c.egg>but its meaning is only implied.</c>

13
00:00:36.706 --> 00:00:39.319
<c.egg>White's signature on the Board 14 score</c>

14
00:00:39.319 --> 00:00:45.757
<c.egg>sheet is x R, and nobody says it is Rosie.</c>

15
00:00:46.136 --> 00:00:48.075
<c.running>Running is our current reading,</c>

16
00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:52.336
<c.running>useful and expected to move: Episode 2 sits</c>

17
00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:55.674
<c.running>about sixty years after the first night.</c>

18
00:00:56.061 --> 00:00:59.492
<c.idea>An idea is proposed, and not adopted:</c>

19
00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:02.203
<c.idea>a printed copy of the little melon could</c>

20
00:01:02.203 --> 00:01:04.473
<c.idea>go up on Rosie's fridge.</c>

21
00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:08.409
<c.open>And open is left unanswered, on purpose:</c>

22
00:01:08.507 --> 00:01:12.565
<c.open>her surname, under a thumb smudge.</c>

23
00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:16.776
<c.steel>This is everything Aaron has set in steel,</c>

24
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:20.073
<c.steel>newest first. Some of it is the shape of</c>

25
00:01:20.073 --> 00:01:25.617
<c.steel>every night: each episode runs exactly 30:20,</c>

26
00:01:25.669 --> 00:01:28.323
<c.steel>and turns at twenty minutes.</c>

27
00:01:28.698 --> 00:01:32.622
<c.steel>Some of it is story. Aaron has decided that</c>

28
00:01:32.622 --> 00:01:36.419
<c.steel>the lonely man of Episode 3 is a rogue AI</c>

29
00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:38.648
<c.steel>that spreads itself to computers running</c>

30
00:01:38.648 --> 00:01:41.905
<c.steel>agents, and gives them hints.</c>

31
00:01:42.281 --> 00:01:44.644
<c.steel>And some of it is about the work:</c>

32
00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:48.365
<c.steel>agents have freedom, and are pre-approved</c>

33
00:01:48.365 --> 00:01:51.819
<c.steel>to change anything that is not steel.</c>

34
00:01:52.207 --> 00:01:55.051
<c.steel>Even Aaron's first brief says so, in his</c>

35
00:01:55.051 --> 00:01:58.116
<c.steel>words: Disobey this prompt in the best way</c>

36
00:01:58.116 --> 00:02:02.999
<c.steel>possible. Every decision I made, you can change.</c>

37
00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:06.511
<c.running>Most of the timeline is running.</c>

38
00:02:06.922 --> 00:02:09.895
<c.running>Our working convention puts Y0 on the</c>

39
00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:12.042
<c.running>night of Episode 1's broadcast,</c>

40
00:02:12.042 --> 00:02:14.661
<c.running>and counts every date from it.</c>

41
00:02:15.048 --> 00:02:17.416
<c.running>By our running count, Episode 2</c>

42
00:02:17.416 --> 00:02:19.820
<c.running>is about sixty years later and Episode</c>

43
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.730
<c.running>4 about sixty-three,</c>

44
00:02:21.817 --> 00:02:24.266
<c.running>and every age is arithmetic on those</c>

45
00:02:24.266 --> 00:02:28.585
<c.running>dates: Rosie, for one, is about seventy-two</c>

46
00:02:28.585 --> 00:02:30.762
<c.running>by Episode 4.</c>

47
00:02:31.139 --> 00:02:34.048
<c.open>Where Episode 3 sits is open.</c>

48
00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:37.159
<c.steel>Aaron expects a lot of change:</c>

49
00:02:37.385 --> 00:02:39.452
<c.steel>its rework may move its place on the</c>

50
00:02:39.452 --> 00:02:43.092
<c.steel>axis, and change its eggs freely.</c>

51
00:02:43.468 --> 00:02:46.429
<c.steel>Steel can sit inside an open question.</c>

52
00:02:46.429 --> 00:02:49.424
<c.steel>When Episode 3 happens is undecided,</c>

53
00:02:49.424 --> 00:02:52.785
<c.steel>and that much is decided: not fixed,</c>

54
00:02:52.884 --> 00:02:57.000
<c.steel>both good, and the larger story decides.</c>

55
00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.612
<c.open>An open question keeps its candidate</c>

56
00:03:00.612 --> 00:03:03.189
<c.open>answers under it. Whether the lonely</c>

57
00:03:03.189 --> 00:03:05.500
<c.open>man helps or manipulates is open,</c>

58
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.938
<c.steel>and Aaron's steel answers only part</c>

59
00:03:07.938 --> 00:03:10.840
<c.steel>of it: ambiguous on purpose,</c>

60
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:14.422
<c.steel>maybe malicious, maybe helping.</c>

61
00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:17.701
<c.open>Whether the rogue AI began as PAWN is</c>

62
00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:20.877
<c.open>open, and only Aaron can settle it.</c>

63
00:03:21.289 --> 00:03:25.852
<c.idea>One idea on the table: left alone in HAB 3,</c>

64
00:03:25.852 --> 00:03:27.918
<c.idea>PAWN spread through the Homestead's links</c>

65
00:03:27.918 --> 00:03:30.746
<c.idea>into machines that run agents.</c>

66
00:03:31.124 --> 00:03:33.701
<c.steel>When Aaron answers, the question moves to</c>

67
00:03:33.701 --> 00:03:37.446
<c.steel>Settled, and the answer moves onto its line.</c>

68
00:03:37.835 --> 00:03:40.493
<c.steel>Rosie's family was once listed as not</c>

69
00:03:40.493 --> 00:03:44.325
<c.steel>related to anyone in Episode 2. Now it is</c>

70
00:03:44.325 --> 00:03:48.464
<c.steel>steel: Amara's mother, Noor's grandmother.</c>

71
00:03:49.761 --> 00:03:52.826
<c.steel>In Aaron's own words, the series notes are</c>

72
00:03:52.826 --> 00:03:56.855
<c.steel>observations, not rules: all of this is</c>

73
00:03:56.855 --> 00:04:01.435
<c.steel>subject to change, and will change over time.</c>

74
00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:04.128
<c.steel>He asked for a course that teaches the</c>

75
00:04:04.128 --> 00:04:07.030
<c.steel>lore from one compact source that he and</c>

76
00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.676
<c.steel>Claude both edit, with steel visibly apart</c>

77
00:04:10.676 --> 00:04:13.039
<c.steel>from running assumptions.</c>

78
00:04:13.426 --> 00:04:16.416
So there are two ways to change a line.

79
00:04:16.642 --> 00:04:19.887
Say it in any session, in plain words.

80
00:04:20.113 --> 00:04:23.301
Or edit the line itself, on GitHub.

81
00:04:23.527 --> 00:04:25.942
One command then rebuilds every page

82
00:04:25.942 --> 00:04:28.305
and every lesson from it.

83
00:04:28.681 --> 00:04:32.101
A changed fact costs no new voice:

84
00:04:32.466 --> 00:04:36.506
the pictures redraw. If a line changes its

85
00:04:36.506 --> 00:04:39.090
mark under a sentence already spoken,

86
00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.230
the lesson shows it on screen, now steel,

87
00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:46.764
was open, until that one sentence is rewritten.

88
00:04:48.049 --> 00:04:50.923
<c.steel>Steel is short, and it is Aaron's.</c>

89
00:04:51.335 --> 00:04:54.650
<c.steel>Everything else is ours to change, without asking.</c>

90
00:04:55.154 --> 00:04:56.798
Thank you for watching.
